e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to compensation;
g. the right to social rehabilitation;
h. the right to complaint handling;
i. the right to assistance and legal aid;
j. the right to obtain visum et repertum free of charge; andk. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process.

Article 26

1. Physical violence as referred to in Article 25 letter a constitutes an act that results in pain accompanied by injury, wounds or disability to the body, miscarriage, fainting and/or causes death of women and/or children.
2. Psychological violence as referred to in Article 25 letter b constitutes an act that results in fear, loss of self-confidence, loss of ability to act, feelings of helplessness and/or psychological suffering to women and/or children.
3. Sexual violence as referred to in Article 25 letter c, includes:a. acts in the form of sexual harassment, consisting of:
1. physical harassment;
2. verbal harassment;
3. gestural harassment;
4. written or pictorial harassment; and
5. psychological/emotional harassment.b. forced sexual intercourse;
c. forced sexual intercourse in an unnatural or unwanted manner; and/ord. forced sexual intercourse with others for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes.
4. Neglect as referred to in Article 25 letter d, includes:a. acts that result in the failure to fulfill the needs of children properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after children as appropriate, committed by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care; and/orc. acts of deliberately neglecting women within the scope of their household, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to an agreement or contract they are obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to such women.
5. Exploitation as referred to in Article 25 letter e, includes:a. acts of exploiting economically or sexually with the intention of benefiting oneself or others;
b. acts with or without the consent of the victim which include but are not limited to prostitution, forced labor or services, slavery or similar practices, oppression, extortion, exploitation of physical, sexual, reproductive organs, or unlawfully removing or transplanting organs and/or body tissues or exploiting the labor or ability of a person by another party to obtain benefits both material and immaterial; andc. all forms of exploitation of sexual organs or other body organs of the victim to obtain benefits, but not limited to prostitution or indecency activities.
6. Other violence as referred to in Article 25 letter f constitutes threats of violence and coercion.
7. Threats of violence as referred to in paragraph (6) include any unlawful act in the form of speech, writing, images, symbols, or body movements, whether with or without using means that cause fear or restrict a person's fundamental freedom.
8. Coercion as referred to in paragraph (6) includes a situation where a person or victim is ordered to do something in such a way that the person does something contrary to their own will.

Article 42

1. Legal aid services as referred to in Article 38 letter a number 4 are to assist victims in undergoing the judicial process. (2) Legal aid services as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by:a. providing legal consultation that includes information regarding the rights of victims and the judicial process;
b. accompanying victims at the investigation, prosecution, and examination levels in court hearings and helping victims to fully explain the violence they experienced; and c. coordinating with fellow law enforcers, volunteer companions, and social workers so that the judicial process proceeds as it should.

Article 28

Child victims of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 27, are granted special rights, as follows:
a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma;
e. the right to obtain freedom; andf. the right to be tried based on the principle of restorative justice.

Article 49

1. The Task Unit for the Protection of Women and Children at Village Level as referred to in Article 45 letter d is a functional work unit that organizes integrated protection services and handling of cases of violence against children and women at the Village level.
2. The Management of the Task Unit for the Protection of Women and Children at Village Level as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:a. elements of Village apparatus;
b. elements of Village or Sub-District Supervisory Non-Commissioned Officers;
c. elements of Community Supervisory Non-Commissioned Officers;
d. community leaders or religious leaders;
e. youth elements; andf. elements of the Village Family Welfare Development Driving Team. (3) The main tasks of the Task Unit for the Protection of Women and Children at Village Level as referred to in paragraph (1) are:a. providing services in the context of protecting women and children;
b. receiving public complaints regarding cases of violence against women and children in the Village area;
c. facilitating women and children who are victims of violence in resolving their problems;
d. socializing to the community about the protection of women and children; ande. providing assistance to victims or referring to the task force for the protection of women and children at Sub-District level.
4. Further provisions regarding the establishment, duties and functions of the task unit for the protection of women and children at village level as referred to in paragraph (1) are stipulated by village head's decree.

