CHAPTER X

CHILD-FRIENDLY REGENCY

Article 56

1. The Regional Government is obligated to implement a Child-Friendly Regency implementation system in order to fulfill children's rights. (2) The development of a Child-Friendly Regency is implemented based on the following principles:a. good governance, namely transparency, accountability, participation, information openness, and rule of law;
b. non-discrimination, namely not differentiating ethnicity, race, religion, gender, language, political views, national origin, economic status, physical or psychological condition of the child, or other factors;
c. the best interests of the child, namely making what is best for the child the primary consideration in every policy, program, and activity;
d. the right to life, survival, and development of the child, namely guaranteeing the right to life, survival and development of the child to the maximum extent possible; ande. respect for the views of the child, namely recognizing and ensuring that every child who has the ability to express their opinion is given the opportunity to express their views freely on all matters affecting them.
3. The implementation instruments of the Child-Friendly Regency as referred to in paragraph (1) include:a. development and public service systems;
b. Child-Friendly Health Services;
c. Child-Friendly Families;
d. Child-Friendly Schools; ande. Child-Friendly Environments;
f. Child-Friendly Islamic Boarding Schools.
4. The Regional Government in implementing the Child-Friendly Regency as referred to in paragraph (1) must pay attention to Child-Friendly Regency Indicators which include:a. institutions; andb. clusters of children's rights.
5. Institutional strengthening as referred to in paragraph (4) letter a includes:a. the existence of laws and regulations and policies for the fulfillment of children's rights;
b. percentage of budget for the fulfillment of children's rights, including budget for institutional strengthening;
c. number of laws and regulations, policies, programs and activities that receive input from Children's Forums and other children's groups;
d. availability of Human Resources (HR) trained in the Convention on the Rights of the Child (CRC) and capable of applying children's rights into policies, programs, and activities;
e. availability of disaggregated child data by gender, age, and sub-district;
f. involvement of community institutions in the fulfillment of children's rights; andg. involvement of the business sector in the fulfillment of children's rights.

Article 58

1. To make the development of a Child-Friendly Regency effective, a Child-Friendly Regency Task Force is formed which is established by Regent's Decree.
2. Membership of the Child-Friendly Regency Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) may come from the Regional Government, children's representatives, the business sector, religious leaders, community leaders and other parties as needed.
3. In carrying out their main duties, members of the Child-Friendly Regency Task Force as referred to in paragraph (2) have detailed tasks including:a. coordinating various efforts to develop a Child-Friendly Regency;
b. preparing a Regional Action Plan for Child-Friendly Regency Development; c. implementing socialization, advocacy and communication for the development of a Child-Friendly Regency;
d. monitoring the implementation of policies, programs and activities in the Regional Action Plan for Child-Friendly Regency Development;
e. conducting evaluation at the end of each year on the implementation of policies, programs and activities in the Regional Action Plan for Child-Friendly Regency Development; andf. submitting reports to the Regent.

Article 24

1. Protection of child victims of mistreatment and neglect as referred to in Article 17 letter g shall be carried out through supervision, prevention, care and rehabilitation efforts by the Regional Government and the community.
2. The implementation of protection for children who are victims of mistreatment and neglect as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in the form of:a. Prevention Services, including:
1. providing protection guarantees for child victims of mistreatment and neglect;
2. providing protection guarantees from all forms of neglect and mistreatment for children;
3. providing socialization and raising awareness of parents and/or the community, the press, educators, the government, law enforcement officials about the adverse effects of mistreatment and neglect; and
4. establishing complaint mechanisms for case handling. b. Risk Reduction Services, including:
1. conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families at risk;
2. providing services for children and families who are victims of neglect and mistreatment including:a. guidance and counseling on parenting patterns and behavior change;
b. legal consultation;
c. mediation to related parties;
d. psychosocial services; ande. temporary shelter. c. Case Handling Services, including:
1. conducting immediate rescue efforts for victims in conditions dangerous to their safety;
2. providing referrals according to the needs of victims;
3. conducting counseling and support from the family;
4. treating children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children; and
5. conducting continuous monitoring and recording of the development of child victims of mistreatment and neglect. (4) The procedures, mechanisms and service standards for special protection for child laborers, child workers and working children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be further regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 22

1. Special Protection for child victims of abduction, sale and trafficking as referred to in Article 17 letter e shall be carried out through supervision, protection, prevention, care and rehabilitation efforts by the Regional Government and the community.
2. The implementation of special protection for children who are victims of abduction, sale and trafficking as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in the form of:a. Prevention Services, including:
1. Providing protection guarantees for child victims of abduction, sale and trafficking; and
2. Providing complaint mechanisms and case handling. b. Risk Reduction Services, including:
1. Conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families at risk; and
2. Providing services for children and victims of abduction, sale and trafficking which include:a. guidance and counseling on parenting patterns and behavior change;
b. legal consultation;
c. mediation to related parties;
d. psychosocial services;
e. economic assistance and life skills;
f. temporary shelter; andg. protecting the identity of victims. c. Case Handling Services, including:
1. Conducting immediate rescue efforts for victims in conditions dangerous to their safety;
2. Providing referrals according to the needs of victims;
3. Conducting counseling and providing support from the family; 4. Treating child victims of abduction, sale and trafficking in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children;
5. Providing accessibility to obtain information regarding case developments;
6. Providing rehabilitation and social reintegration services; and 7. Creating a database of child victims of abduction, sale and trafficking.

Article 14

Child protection in the field of Health as referred to in Article 10  letter c, includes:
a. providing facilities and organizing comprehensive health efforts  for children, so that every child obtains an optimal degree of health  from the womb carried out by the Regional  Government;
b. organizing comprehensive and free health efforts for  all children including those from poor families;
c. providing health insurance for child victims of violence, exploitation,  neglect and abuse;
d. being responsible for maintaining health and caring for children from the  womb carried out by family and parents; ande. ensuring that children born are protected from diseases that threaten  survival and or cause disability carried out by  the Regional Government, community, family and parents.

Article 45

To assist women's empowerment and child protection, the Regional Government may establish:
a. Regional Technical Implementation Unit for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;
b. Integrated Service Center for Women's Empowerment and Children at the Regional level;
c. Task Force for Women's and Child Protection at the Sub-district Level; d. Task Unit for Women's and Child Protection at the Village Level;
e. Regional Indonesian Child Protection Commission;
f. Regional Children's Forum; and g. Other institutions or organizations in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 62

1. Reporting on the implementation of women's empowerment and child protection shall be conducted by:a. P2TP2A to the Regent;
b. Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children at Sub-District Level to the Sub-District Head;
